 then filtering data and  what is a boolean condition  after that  to go around with good data practices or  integrate their work into  spreadsheet life  how do you save out this stuff into an  excel sheet so at least part of your  data pipeline is reproducible and so now  we have to show a another function that  does something else we have to show them  we sort of go back and say like hey  remember you can always save these  intermediates into other outputs  and then we've used this other function  to save this out into excel file and  then they can carry on or give that to  another colleague or if they don't know  how to do plotting yet  they can plot their data but then this  way if the data set updates they don't  have to worry about that part of the  analysis  the next thing so i focus a lot around  tidy data concepts how do we go from  data set to the left to the data set on  the right well then they're gonna have  to know about  uh lists vectors or different ways you  can uh select code so that's like tidy  select in the r r example  um you have to recognize is this a  pivot longer or pivot wider operation or  melt uh cast operation in python it's  going to be melt and pivot table et  cetera et cetera they're going to have  to identify that  and then at the end they might want to  do a little bit of cleanup so i show  them the drop n a function or you might  need to drop missing variables just so  you have a clean data set  from the plotting perspective so we go  back to say hey plotting is another  library so here's ggplot2 there is the  plotting syntax that uses pluses instead  of a piping notation but what's really  important in the context of ggplot is  the entire system of how layers work so  we have our data layer our mapping layer  where we put our aesthetic and then we  have a stats layer and then a geometry  layer etc etc or a theme layer and that  all of that information needs to get  conveyed  on top of that  you also have to show them that  different variables in our data set  use different types of plots it for  example it wouldn't make sense to create  a scatter plot of just the column of  eight right we need two  continuous variables to create a scatter  plot  and then last but not least how do we go  about fitting a model  i have a background in epidemiology and  public health and one of the core  methods that we use is logistic  regression so i sort of  show them  our learners logistic regression because  most of my learners or my audience is  people or are people in the health  sphere  once you fit the model  you have to know or in order to fit a  model you have to know about this  formula syntax and then the rest of the  bits and pieces to  put your information into a function so  that's a little bit of a review  looking at your model results now we  have to use a summary function how do  you interpret it  how do you get the coefficients out as a  data frame so there's another library we  can use broom and a tidy function from  broom to get our coefficient table  and then we can go back to some of the  stuff we learned in the very beginning  when we were exploring data  where in order to interpret logistic  regression output we actually have to  exponentiate our coefficients and  interpret them as odds ratios instead of  log odds and so there's another bit of  information or another data process that  they would have to do in order to  interpret their coefficients  and so a big part of this and some of  the things i guess like i found the  words to describe it is  data science is different from computer  science education  but what exactly makes it different  and  if we look for example at a typical  computer science question um this is  asking you about binary search from a  five element array  i can read you this question i can read  this question i still have no idea what  this question is asking for but i'm sure  if you have a formal computer science  education uh this will make more sense i  also remember  taking uber in san francisco many years  ago and literally like got interview  questions in the uber ride and was  around stuff like this which isn't data  science related  and so  this is why um this is one of the  differences between  computer science and data science but  then what is fundamentally the  difference here  well  what i think is  in a lot of computer science courses  that you take a data frame object isn't  really a data structure that you learn  about you pretty much learn about lists  and then everything goes around lists if  you're in a python world then you learn  about dictionaries and and all of that  stuff um so that's why stuff like  indexing and for loops and all of that  become  core computer science education topics  but if you look at the r for data  science book like iteration and for  loops are like at the very end of the  book it's almost like if you're working  with a data frame object this probably  your last resort or you don't really  need to do this because you can apply  things apply functions on a data frame  and so because a data frame is so  fundamental to  data science  the way data science needs to get taught  should be different or the ordering of  topics should be different from computer  science education  at the end of the day a lot of the stuff  from a typical intro computer science  class probably will you'll learn as a  data science but it's really about this  ordering ordering and trying to make the  beginning easier for people to pick up  and so if we compare  existing books so there's offer data  science  outside so we saw this morning from  danielle's talk  you can use data visualization as a  means to drive or motivate people  since some most of the stuff that i'm  working on this is all workshop  materials i'm going to sort of assume  that  you are already self-motivated enough to  attend this workshop so i don't need to  motivate you more in the beginning  because you are signing up for a  workshop so that's why i can push tidy  data principles higher because i'm  saving um some of that time from  plotting for later on  but from r for data science they put  plotting first some workflow stuff and  then they also do tidy data pretty fast  if you uh filter down that um table of  contents for the things that are  actually like chapters there's another  book data science for javascript that's  written by greg wilson toby hodges and  maya gans  they are teaching  javascript so you'll see in the  beginning of this book it's a lot of dom  manipulation like traditional javascript  stuff and then the actual data  manipulation stuff happens towards the  end um that is sort of like a  uh  style and you're just assuming that like  wait we actually need to teach like  something that shows up on the browser  before we actually load in the data set  so they know what they're doing so i can  get that and then the ultra open intro  statistics textbook doesn't really cover  tidy data principles in the beginning  but at least they are setting up  fundamental data principles  for a statistics context  from the python world you have wes's  python for data analysis book and if you  sort of like go past the first 150 pages  which is the first four four and a half  chapters um  it  it essentially covers data wrangling  which is that pivoting operation really  fast it's essentially the second chapter  in the book um learning the pandas  library is a book by  matt harrison and  he comes from a computer science  background so there's a lot of stuff  about just the actual data structure  about  data frames and  series and then eventually we go into  processing data with pivoting and tidy  data stuff  and then for the stuff that i work on in  pandas for everyone  tidy data comes up as chapter six and  that's also because this is supposed to  be a more general book so i try to put  plotting as early as i can and that's  why plotting ends up being chapter three  in ds4 biomed  the materials are central uh the first  thing i need to get is let's start with  spreadsheets and get your spreadsheet  correct i essentially teach tidy data  principles in spreadsheets they just  don't know it yet and then by  chapter six or part six of the book we  actually show you like the the ways you  can fix up spreadsheets if that's the  way you're inputting data and then if i  were to teach a conference workshop i  literally just throw tidy data as a  second thing  this is also because there's a huge time  constraint in conference workshop so  that is uh the second thing i show you  because it's more  i would say it's like a harder topic to  grasp and that's why that just gets  pushed up to the very top  and so  what do i want you to get out of this  talk um  identify if you do end up creating your  own set of learning materials identify  who your learners are figure out what  they want to know and then plan a guided  learning track and then the way you can  sort of  figure out what your learners know you  can if you want use this survey that i  have created um what's next or really  like what is the rest of my dissertation  that i need to finish by december um is  the actual survey validation for this so  it actually becomes a validated tool for  other people to use um there is  all of that stuff that i talked about  learning objectives and how do we um  assess them um there are pre-post stuff  so there are  workshops that are involved uh which i  will post into the conference chat right  after my talk that if you want you can  sign up for and then there's also a  long-term retention  uh component to this like if i teach you  this now do you  is it useful enough that you stick with  it or have you used it four months six  months down the line and then there's  another thing that sort of builds on the  stuff that i worked on with grade this  about different types of formative  assessments like if i were to ask you a  question in the middle of class should i  give you a question and a blank  canvas like a blank text box or should i  fill in and have you fill in like um  blanks for code that works  um for additional resources so there's  carawoo's  spreadsheet paper if you want to learn  about personas there's some links about  personas and if you are an educator  there are some introductory stuff about  bloom's taxonomy and like teaching  techniques of how to  sort of put words to  what teachers do in the classroom  so with that thank you everyone the  slides and links and repositories are  all posted up on github and if you  really want you can read the prelim that  i um have successfully like gone through  in february there's a link to that as  well  great thank you very much dan  and i can't believe the first morning is  already over  before we go to lunch
 

